    "content": "<p><componentpreview name=\"alert-1\" title=\"An alert with an icon, title and description.\" description=\"An alert with an icon, title and description. The title says &#x27;Heads up!&#x27; and the description is &#x27;You can add components to your app using the cli.&#x27;.\"></componentpreview></p>\n<h2>Philosophy</h2>\n<p>Alerts exist to break the user's flow -- intentionally. When something demands attention, a subtle inline message beats a disruptive modal every time. We keep the API to just <code>variant</code> because an alert's job is simple: show a message with visual weight. Anything more complex belongs in a dialog.</p>\n<h2>How It's Built</h2>\n<p>&#x3C;MermaidDiagram chart={<code>graph TD   Alert[Alert]   Alert --> variants[\"@gentleduck/variants\"]:::internal   Alert --> libs[\"@gentleduck/libs\"]:::internal   variants --> |\"provides\"| VariantSystem[\"Variant System\\n(default, destructive)\"]   Alert --> |\"composes\"| AlertTitle[\"AlertTitle\"]   Alert --> |\"composes\"| AlertDescription[\"AlertDescription\"]   classDef internal fill:#2dd4bf,stroke:#14b8a6,color:#fff </code>} /></p>\n<h2>Installation</h2>\n<tabs defaultvalue=\"cli\">\n<tabslist>\n  <tabstrigger value=\"cli\">CLI</tabstrigger>\n  <tabstrigger value=\"manual\">Manual</tabstrigger>\n</tabslist>\n<tabscontent value=\"cli\">\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx @gentleduck/cli add alert\n</code></pre>\n</tabscontent>\n<tabscontent value=\"manual\">\n<steps>\n<p><step>Install the following dependencies:</step></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install @gentleduck/variants @gentleduck/libs\n</code></pre>\n<p><step>Copy and paste the following code into your project.</step></p>\n<componentsource name=\"alert\">\n<p><step>Update the import paths to match your project setup.</step></p>\n</componentsource></steps>\n</tabscontent>\n</tabs>\n<h2>Usage</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { Alert, AlertDescription, AlertTitle } from \"@/components/ui/alert\"\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">&#x3C;Alert>\n  &#x3C;Terminal className=\"h-4 w-4\" />\n  &#x3C;AlertTitle>Heads up!&#x3C;/AlertTitle>\n  &#x3C;AlertDescription>\n    You can add components and dependencies to your app using the cli.\n  &#x3C;/AlertDescription>\n&#x3C;/Alert>\n</code></pre>\n<h2>API Reference</h2>\n<h3>Alert</h3>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>variant</code></td><td><code>'default' | 'destructive'</code></td><td><code>'default'</code></td><td>Visual style variant for the alert</td></tr><tr><td><code>className</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>--</td><td>Additional CSS classes applied to the alert container</td></tr><tr><td><code>children</code></td><td><code>React.ReactNode</code></td><td>--</td><td>Alert content, typically an icon, AlertTitle, and AlertDescription</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Also accepts all standard <code>React.ComponentProps&#x3C;'div'></code> props.</em></p>\n<h3>AlertTitle</h3>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>className</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>--</td><td>Additional CSS classes applied to the title element</td></tr><tr><td><code>children</code></td><td><code>React.ReactNode</code></td><td>--</td><td>Title text content</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Also accepts all standard <code>React.ComponentProps&#x3C;'div'></code> props.</em></p>\n<h3>AlertDescription</h3>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n<table><thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>className</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>--</td><td>Additional CSS classes applied to the description element</td></tr><tr><td><code>children</code></td><td><code>React.ReactNode</code></td><td>--</td><td>Description text content</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><em>Also accepts all standard <code>React.ComponentProps&#x3C;'div'></code> props.</em></p>",
