    "component": true,
    "content": "<p>We do not ship any typography styles by default. This page is an example of how you can use utility classes to style your text.</p>\n<p><componentpreview name=\"typography-1\" description=\"A collection of typographic elements.\" class=\"[&#x26;_.preview]:!h-auto\" hidecode=\"\"></componentpreview></p>\n<h2>Philosophy</h2>\n<p>Typography isn't a component -- it's a system. Rather than wrapping every HTML heading and paragraph in a React component, we provide utility classes that style native elements consistently. This keeps your markup semantic, your bundle lean, and your styling flexible. Use <code>&#x3C;h1></code> with a class, not <code>&#x3C;Heading level={1}></code>.</p>\n<h2>h1</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-2\">\n<h2>h2</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-3\">\n<h2>h3</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-4\">\n<h2>h4</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-5\">\n<h2>p</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-6\">\n<h2>blockquote</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-7\">\n<h2>table</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-8\">\n<h2>list</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-9\">\n<h2>Inline code</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-10\">\n<h2>Lead</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-11\">\n<h2>Large</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-12\">\n<h2>Small</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-13\">\n<h2>Muted</h2>\n<componentpreview name=\"typography-14\"></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview></componentpreview>",
