    "content": "<p>&#x3C;MermaidDiagram chart={<code>flowchart TD     A[npx gentleduck/cli] --> B[init]     A --> C[add]     B --> D[Configure Project]     C --> E[Select Components]     E --> F[Install Dependencies]     F --> G[Copy Source Files] </code>} /></p>\n<h2>Philosophy</h2>\n<p>A good CLI makes adoption frictionless. duck-cli doesn't install pre-built packages -- it copies source code into your project. This means you own every line, can modify anything, and never wait for a library release to fix a bug. The tradeoff is intentional: full control over zero-config convenience.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>init</h2>\n<p>Use the <code>init</code> command to initialize configuration and dependencies for a new project.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx @gentleduck/cli init [components...]\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Options</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">Usage: @gentleduck/cli init [options] [components...]\n\ninit the project\n\nArguments:\n  [components...]   names, url or local path to component to install. When not provided, you’ll be directed to a list to select from.\n\nOptions:\n  -y, --yes           skip confirmation prompt. (default: false)\n  -c, --cwd &#x3C;cwd>     the working directory. defaults to the current directory. (default: process.cwd())\n  -f, --force         overwrite existing components. (default: false)\n</code></pre>\n<hr>\n<h2>add</h2>\n<p>Use the <code>add</code> command to add components and dependencies to your project.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx @gentleduck/cli add [components...]\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Options</strong></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">Usage: @gentleduck/cli add [options] [components...]\n\nadd components to your project\n\nArguments:\n  [components...]   names, url or local path to component to install. When not provided, you’ll be directed to a list to select from.\n\nOptions:\n  -y, --yes           skip confirmation prompt. (default: false)\n  -f, --force         overwrite existing components. (default: false)\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code></code></pre>",
